window.docContents[122].resolve({"/ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics/#iso80000-part8":{"contents":"The sixth part specified in full is ISO 80000-8, Acoustics. Every item — all of\n8-1 … 8-17, including the sub-suffixed pressure pair (8-2.1, 8-2.2) — is catalogued: the\nlogarithmic frequency range, static and sound pressure, the sound-particle kinematics\n(displacement, velocity, acceleration), volume flow rate, the sound-energy cluster\n(energy density, energy, power, intensity), sound exposure, the two impedances, the three\nlogarithmic levels, and the reverberation time — each carrying its exact source as data:\nthe part, the printed item designation, the principal quantity symbol, and the coherent\nSI unit symbol. Only citation locators are recorded; no normative content from the\nlicensed standard is reproduced. The defining mathematics of selected remarks is\nspecified in the sibling module Part8.DefiningRelations.\n\nAcoustics brings two faces of the catalogue's central thesis, and — unusually — its\nboundary. The logarithmic levels (sound pressure level, sound power level, sound\nexposure level) are each 10·lg(quantity / reference), ratios on a logarithmic scale\nreported in the decibel: dimension one, three distinct kinds the dimension functor\ncollapses to a single point. A genuine dimension collision has sound pressure and sound\nenergy density carry the same dimension M·L⁻¹·T⁻² — force per area equals energy per\nvolume — while staying distinct kinds. And the boundary: the two homonymous\nimpedances are told apart by their dimension, the case where the necessary\ndiscriminator is, this time, also sufficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"26. ISO 80000-8 — Acoustics","id":"/ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics/#iso80000-part8"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Questions-to-ask-the-model-so-far":{"contents":"* Question* Check* What the verdict means\n* Can oscillator B's mass enter A's bare-real frequency?* bare_real_cross_oscillators* Yes; the expression has type ℝ.\n* Does kinding alone prevent the cross-oscillator quotient?* kinded_bearer_free_cross_oscillators* No; the registered springConstantK / massK edge accepts the two Quantity values.\n* Are oscillator A and B different sorts?* oscillator_context_and_sort* No; both have sort harmonic oscillator.\n* Can both oscillators be given one constant designation?* designation* The function is not injective, so it cannot witness Designated Oscillator.\n* Does the shared oscillator index admit its own k/m and refuse cross-oscillator k/m?* oscillator_indexed_quantities, refused_cross_oscillator* Yes; same-oscillator division is accepted, while A's stiffness over B's mass is refused.\n* Does the object index keep their masses apart?* refused_cross_oscillator* Yes; assignment and addition across A and B are refused.\n* Can two weighings of oscillator A at different times be compared on R₁?* same_rig_estimate_difference* Yes; the typed difference has magnitude (later.osc .A).m - (earlier.osc .A).m.\n* Can same-named oscillator A masses from R₁ and R₂ be added when their parameters are identical?* refused_cross_oscillator* No; OscillatorOf .R₁ and OscillatorOf .R₂ are distinct bearers by nominal identity.\n* Can a nominal name reach a dedicated kind?* refused_unsorted_object* Not without a sort; the nominal Object has no Sorted instance.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.9. Questions to ask the model so far","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Questions-to-ask-the-model-so-far"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.1. Synopsis","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis"}});